  "body": "What happens: the reach laws decide \"upright\" with an exact float compare (`is_vertical` in `crates/sim/src/verify/`). A move whose axis is off vertical by rounding noise (about 1e-10 degrees, as a rotated setup or a posed operation can produce) takes the tilted-tool path, and a holder wider than the cutter then flips to a collision the upright path would not report. Found by the high-tier re-review of PR #843 (https://github.com/NeoTech/Camcraft/pull/843#pullrequestreview-5866435965, \"older bug, not from this PR\"). For the shanks #843 newly checks the reviewer measured no such flip up to about 0.015 degrees.\n\nWhy it matters: not a silent pass - a false collision that blocks a good program, and one that appears or disappears with floating-point noise.\n\nExpected: \"upright\" is decided with a stated angular tolerance, and a tilt inside it is judged exactly as a vertical move; a real small tilt (above the tolerance) is judged as tilted. The tolerance and why it is safe (how far a band can move sideways at that angle over the tool's length, against the gouge tolerance) go in `docs/decisions/`.\n\nAlso from the same review, smaller: a tilted tool with both its shank and its holder in the part reports the same `ShankHit` twice for one move - de-duplicate per move and group.\n\nDone means: a test with a holder wider than the cutter gets the same verdict at 0 and at 1e-10 degrees of tilt, and a tilted verdict at a real small tilt; the duplicate hit is gone; no existing verdict or golden changes. Tests live in `crates/sim/tests` where no app fixture is needed.\n",
